self-reference-bhartrhari-and-lawvere-source = lines-of (cons "# Self-reference: Bhartṛhari and Lawvere" (cons "" (cons (quoted (cons "Raised by Avik on 2026-11-18 (10:03 UTC): " (cons "Lawvere, tell me about this. I've studied the fixed-point theorem's" nil))) (cons "deeply of course and I have no trouble with such things per my foundations in jain/buddhist logic and direct" (cons (quoted (cons "experience. but what's Bhartṛhari?" (cons " Then (10:08 UTC): " (cons "knowledge graph node(s) for this pair" (cons ". The pair: one" nil))))) (cons "statement about self-reference, made in India in the fifth century in the theory of language and in Europe in" (cons "1969 in category theory." (cons "" (cons "## Lawvere's fixed-point theorem (1969)" (cons "" (cons (quoted (cons "F. William Lawvere, " (cons "Diagonal arguments and cartesian closed categories" (cons " (Lecture Notes in Mathematics 92," nil)))) (cons "1969). In a cartesian closed category: if there is a map φ : A → B^A that is point-surjective (every map" (cons "A → B is φ(a) for some point a), then every map f : B → B has a fixed point. The proof is one line: take" (cons "g(a) = f(φ(a)(a)); g = φ(a₀) for some a₀; then g(a₀) = f(g(a₀))." (cons "" (cons "Read contrapositively: if B has a map without a fixed point, no A can reach all of B^A. Each classical" (cons "limitative result is an instance:" (cons "" (cons "- Cantor (1891): B = 2, f = negation; no set surjects onto its power set." (cons "- Russell (1901): the set of all sets that are not members of themselves." (cons "- Gödel (1931) and Tarski (1933): B = sentences or truth values, f = negation; a sufficiently expressive" (cons "  language cannot define its own truth." (cons "- Turing (1936): B = halts / does not halt, f = the machine that does the opposite; no program decides" (cons "  halting for all programs." (cons "" (cons "What all of them say: a system that can name its own naming meets a fixed point it cannot escape by" (cons "negation." (cons "" (cons "## Bhartṛhari (5th century)" (cons "" (cons (quoted (cons "Grammarian and philosopher of language in Pāṇini's tradition; his work is the Vākyapadīya, " (cons "Treatise on" nil))) (cons (quoted (cons "Sentences and Words" (cons ". His positions:" nil))) (cons "" (cons "- No cognition comes without the word: knowing is shot through with language, not labelled by it" (cons "  afterwards." (cons "- The sentence is the unit of meaning; words are abstractions cut out of sentences; meaning is grasped" (cons "  whole, in one flash, while the sounds that reveal it arrive in order." (cons "- The Word is the ground of reality: distinctions among things are distinctions of language." (cons (quoted (cons "- The paradox. Some schools (the Naiyāyikas: " (cons "whatever is, is knowable and nameable" (cons ") held that" nil)))) (cons "  everything can be named; Buddhist philosophers held that some things cannot. Bhartṛhari saw that the" (cons "  unnameability thesis cannot be verified by an instance, since giving the instance names what it calls" (cons "  unnameable. He applies it to two relations: the relation of signification cannot itself be signified," (cons "  and the relation of inherence cannot be signified. The same text treats the liar paradox family." (cons "- The exit. He identifies a hidden parameter whose neglect produces the paradox and whose restoration" (cons "  dissolves it: the act of signifying and its extension in time. The paradox (contradiction, or infinite" (cons "  regress) arises when that act is abstracted from its moment by assuming a simultaneous, opposite" (cons (quoted (cons "  function. Signifying is an act at a moment; the act that names " (cons "the unnameable" (cons " is not the act it" nil)))) (cons "  names." (cons "" (cons "## The literature" (cons "" (cons (quoted (cons "- Hans G. Herzberger and Radhika Herzberger, " (cons "Bhartrhari's Paradox" (cons ", Journal of Indian Philosophy 9:1" nil)))) (cons "  (March 1981), 1–17; reprinted in Studies in Indian Philosophy (L. D. Institute of Indology, Ahmedabad)." (cons "  They develop his remarks into a paradox in the modern sense and connect it directly to Russell's paradox" (cons "  and to the axiom of regularity, by which axiomatic set theory forbids sets that are members of" (cons "  themselves." (cons (quoted (cons "- Jan E. M. Houben, " (cons "Paradox and perspectivism in the philosophy of language of Bhartṛhari" (cons ": his hidden" nil)))) (cons "  parameter suits Barwise and Etchemendy's solution to the liar (The Liar, 1989), where a proposition is" (cons "  evaluated in a situation." (cons "- A later reply (PhilPapers record PARBOW) argues that the appeal to set theory does not by itself settle" (cons "  his claim about signification, and that arguments akin to the semantic paradoxes, such as the liar, come" (cons "  much closer." (cons "" (cons "## The correspondence" (cons "" (cons (quoted (cons "- " (cons "The relation of signification cannot be signified" (cons " is Tarski's undefinability in the theory of" nil)))) (cons "  language: a language cannot contain its own semantics. In Lawvere's terms, words A, the properties of" (cons "  words B^A, negation the fixed-point-free map on B; calling a thing unnameable forces the fixed point," (cons "  because the call names it." (cons "- The two exits differ. Set theory forbids the self-reference (regularity). Bhartṛhari keeps it and indexes" (cons "  it to its occurrence: meaning is relative to the act and its moment. That is the receive relation of" (cons "  Part I (meaning depends on the receiver's state), and it is homotopy type theory's way, which keeps" (cons "  self-application and makes truth a construction in context, not set theory's prohibition." (cons "- Fourteen centuries lie between Bhartṛhari and Russell (indian-knowledge-history)." nil))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))
